  "definition": {
   "definition": "RBI defines a certificate of deposit as a negotiable, unsecured money-market instrument issued by a bank as a usance promissory note against funds deposited at the bank for a maturity of up to one year. CDs may be issued only by Scheduled Commercial Banks, Regional Rural Banks and Small Finance Banks, to residents in India, and only in dematerialised form held with a SEBI-registered depository. Minimum denomination is ₹5 lakh and multiples of ₹5 lakh thereafter, and the tenor at issuance must be not less than seven days and not more than one year. These rules sit in the Master Direction — RBI (Certificate of Deposit) Directions, 2021, issued under sections 45W/45U of the RBI Act, 1934.",
   "source_name": "RBI Master Direction — Reserve Bank of India (Certificate of Deposit) Directions, 2021",
   "source_url": "https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=12108",
